Write implementation.kt directly from this prompt without exploring outside the workspace and without long deliberation: nothing can be compiled or run while generating, no other project's files are reference material, and the design below is already decided.
Generated Kotlin ABI (package real.posting.client, all public data classes with positional primary constructors): Header(name: kotlin.String, value: kotlin.String); Request(method: HttpMethod, url: kotlin.String, headers: cott_runtime.CottList<Header>, body: kotlin.String, timeout_ms: kotlin.UInt); Response(status: kotlin.UShort, url: kotlin.String, headers: cott_runtime.CottList<Header>, body: kotlin.String); sealed interface HttpMethod with objects Get, Head, Post, Put, Patch, Delete, Options and data class Custom(name: kotlin.String); sealed interface PostingError with data classes InvalidArguments(message), InvalidRequest(message) and NetworkFailed(message), each taking one kotlin.String; real.posting.client.MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_BYTES is a kotlin.ULong. cott_runtime.CottList(iterable) copies any Iterable and is itself a kotlin.collections.List; results are cott_runtime.Ok(value) and cott_runtime.Err(error). The facade treats any thrown exception as a contract violation, so catch every java.io.IOException and RuntimeException inside the implementation and return NetworkFailed.
Decided design of send_request: (1) Validate first and return InvalidRequest before any I/O: timeout_ms == 0u; URL prefix; an explicit character scan of the whole URL that rejects every char that is not an RFC 3986 character (unreserved, reserved gen-delims/sub-delims, and '%' only when followed by two hex digits) -- java.net.URI accepts raw non-ASCII, so never rely on URI for this; a non-empty host (parse authority by hand: skip userinfo up to the last '@', host is up to ':' or the closing ']' of an IP literal, port digits only and <= 65535 when present); header names non-empty HTTP tokens; header values without CR or LF; Custom method name an HTTP token.
(2) Transport: java.net.Socket and javax.net.ssl.SSLSocketFactory (https) with a hand-written HTTP/1.1 writer and parser; java.net.HttpURLConnection cannot send Custom or PATCH methods and java.net.http.HttpClient rejects Host and Content-Length headers, so neither is used. Connect with Socket.connect(InetSocketAddress, timeout_ms) and set Socket.soTimeout to timeout_ms. Request target is the URL path plus '?' and the query exactly as written (keep an empty '?'; '/' when the path is empty; drop the fragment; never re-encode or decode percent-escapes). Send Host as the URL host exactly as written (case kept, IPv6 literal with its brackets, userinfo ignored) with ':port' only when the port is not the scheme default, unless the headers being sent contain a Host header (ASCII case-insensitive); on a request after a cross-origin redirect the Host header was stripped, so the URL's own host is sent; then the headers in order, Content-Length only when the body is non-empty, Connection: close, then the UTF-8 body.
(3) Response: parse the status line strictly (anything not starting with HTTP/1. and a 3-digit status is NetworkFailed), header lines in received order with duplicates kept (a header line without a colon is NetworkFailed; split at the first colon, trim the value), then the body: none for HEAD or statuses 1xx/204/304, chunked when Transfer-Encoding says chunked, else Content-Length, else read to close. Count body bytes against MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_BYTES while reading and return NetworkFailed when exceeded. A connection closed inside the headers, inside a body of known length, or before the last chunk is NetworkFailed. A final status outside 100-599 is NetworkFailed.
(4) UTF-8 decoding: new String(bytes, UTF_8) and CharsetDecoder emit a single U+FFFD for an encoded surrogate ED A0..BF xx, where Unicode maximal-subpart replacement needs three (ED A0 80 -> three U+FFFD), so write a small hand-written decoder over the ByteArray. Well-formed sequences are exactly 00..7F; C2..DF 80..BF; E0 A0..BF 80..BF; E1..EC 80..BF 80..BF; ED 80..9F 80..BF; EE..EF 80..BF 80..BF; F0 90..BF 80..BF 80..BF; F1..F3 80..BF 80..BF 80..BF; F4 80..8F 80..BF 80..BF. At each position read a lead byte; when it is a valid lead, consume continuation bytes one at a time while each is in the range allowed at that position; if the sequence is complete emit the code point, otherwise emit ONE U+FFFD for the consumed prefix (the valid lead plus accepted continuations) and resume at the offending byte without consuming it; a byte that is not a valid lead (80..C1, F5..FF) is one U+FFFD by itself; ED followed by A0..BF stops after ED, so each of the three bytes is its own U+FFFD. Keep a BOM (EF BB BF -> U+FEFF) and NUL characters unchanged.
(5) Redirects: at most 10 for Get and Head on statuses 301, 302, 303, 307, 308 that carry a Location header. Never use java.net.URI.resolve for Location: it implements RFC 2396 (base http://h/dir/redir?x=1 with "?q=1" gives http://h/dir/?q=1, and http://h/redir with "../../echo" gives http://h/../../echo). Hand-write RFC 3986 section 5.2: split the reference into scheme/authority/path/query/fragment, merge paths (base path up to and including its last '/'; empty reference path keeps the base path and, when the reference has no query, the base query), run remove_dot_segments (section 5.2.4; empty segments are kept, '..' above the root is dropped), keep the reference fragment. A Location containing any character outside the URL character set of step (1) (including '%' not followed by two hex digits), or whose resolved scheme is not http/https, is not followed and the 3xx response is returned. Do not follow when the target leaves https for http. Compare origins with the scheme and host lower-cased and default ports (80/443) filled in; when the origin changes drop Authorization, Cookie, Proxy-Authorization and Host (ASCII case-insensitive) from the headers sent afterwards. Response.url is request.url unchanged when no redirect was followed (never normalized); after a followed redirect it is the resolved Location including its fragment. The base fragment is never inherited. Examples that must hold: base http://h/dir/redir with "?q=1" -> http://h/dir/redir?q=1; base http://h/redir with "../../echo" -> http://h/echo; base http://h/a/b/c with "..//g" -> http://h/a//g.
